L000084	\begin{abstract}
L000085	Discrete dynamics arise naturally in systems with broken temporal translation symmetry and are typically described by first-order recurrence relations representing classical or quantum Markov chains.
L000086	When memory effects induced by hidden degrees of freedom are relevant, however, higher-order discrete evolution equations are generally required.
L000087	Focusing on linear dynamics, we identify a well-delineated weak-memory regime where such equations can, on an intermediate time scale, be systematically reduced to a unique first-order counterpart acting on the same state space.
L000088	We formulate our results as a mathematical theorem and work out two examples showing how they can be applied to stochastic Floquet dynamics under coarse-grained and quantum collisional models.
L000089	\end{abstract}
L000090	
L000091	
L000092	\maketitle
L000093	
L000094	The dynamical principles of classical and quantum mechanics imply that physical systems across all scales evolve continuously in time.
L000095	Nonetheless, processes with broken temporal translation symmetry can often be naturally understood as sequences of discrete steps.
L000096	Isolated systems subject to rapidly oscillating fields, for example, admit a stroboscopic description in terms of time-independent effective Floquet Hamiltonians that can be engineered through the applied driving, making it possible to realize phenomena with no equilibrium counterparts~\cite{bukovUniversalHighfrequencyBehavior2015,
L000097	eckardtColloquiumAtomicQuantum2017,
L000098	okaFloquetEngineeringQuantum2019}.
L000099	In open systems, periodic driving typically leads to the formation of limit cycles, where state variables converge to stable fixed points in stroboscopic time~\cite{moskaletsFloquetScatteringTheory2002,
L000100	rayStochasticThermodynamicsPeriodically2017,
L000101	menczelLimitCyclesPeriodically2019,
L000102	moriFloquetStatesOpen2023}.
L000103	This behavior arises from dissipation balancing energy absorption and can be observed, for instance, in mesoscopic devices such as stochastic pumps, which generate directed currents through cyclic variations of control parameters~\cite{sinitsynUniversalGeometricTheory2007,
L000104	rahavDirectedFlowNonadiabatic2008,
L000105	espositoStochasticThermodynamicsHidden2015,
L000106	razMimickingNonequilibriumSteady2016,
L000107	funoShortcutsAdiabaticPumping2020}, see Fig.~\ref{fig:Int}.
L000108	
L000109	Besides arising naturally from periodic modulations of continuous dynamics, discrete evolution equations also serve as powerful tools to disentangle the complexity of systems with large numbers of interacting degrees of freedom.
L000110	Discrete-time lattice models, such as cellular automata and quantum circuits, for instance, offer elegant frameworks to explore how macroscopic features of many-body systems such as entanglement spreading and hydrodynamic behavior emerge from local interactions~\cite{nahumQuantumEntanglementGrowth2017,
L000111	nahumOperatorSpreadingRandom2018,
L000112	vonkeyserlingkOperatorHydrodynamicsOTOCs2018,
L000113	khemaniOperatorSpreadingEmergence2018,
L000114	chanSolutionMinimalModel2018,
L000115	rakovszkyDiffusiveHydrodynamicsOutofTimeOrdered2018,
L000116	rakovszkySubballisticGrowthRenyi2019,
L000117	bertiniEntanglementSpreadingMinimal2019,
L000118	klobasExactThermalizationDynamics2021,
L000119	klobasEntanglementDynamicsRule2021,
L000120	klobasExactRelaxationGibbs2021,
L000121	bucaRule54ExactlySolvableModel2021,
L000122	bertiniExactQuenchDynamics2024,
L000123	kimCircuitsSimplePlatform2025}.
L000124	In a similar spirit, collisional models enable an elegant description of open quantum systems, even beyond the weak-coupling regime, by replacing continuous environments with discrete collections of ancillas that interact sequentially with the observable
L000125	system~\cite{karevskiQuantumNonequilibriumSteady2009,
L000126	strasbergQuantumInformationThermodynamics2017,
L000127	strasbergRepeatedInteractionsQuantum2019,
L000128	seahCollisionalQuantumThermometry2019,
L000129	rodriguesThermodynamicsWeaklyCoherent2019}, see Fig.~\ref{fig:Int}.
L000130	
L000131	\begin{figure}
L000132	\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{F1.pdf}
L000133	\caption{\textbf{Top:}
L000134	Mesoscopic charge pump.
L000135	The device consists of two quantum dots, each coupled to a thermochemical reservoir, and admits three microstates corresponding to the left dot, right dot, or neither being occupied.
L000136	Charge pumping proceeds in a three-stroke cycle: a particle is absorbed by the left dot (1a), tunnels between the dots (1b), and is ejected from the right dot (1c), with respective probabilities $L_+$, $L_0$ and $L_-$.
L000137	An observer that measures the total charge of the system distinguishes two mesostates, whose dynamics are non-Markovian.
L000138	\textbf{Bottom:}
L000139	Collisional model with memory.
L000140	A quantum system $S$ interacts sequentially with a stream of identical ancillas $A_i$.
L000141	Ancilla $A_1$ first collides with the system (2a), then with $A_2$ (2b), before leaving the scattering region (2c).
L000142	System-ancilla and ancilla-ancilla interactions are described by bipartite maps $\mU_i$ and $\mQ_{ij}$, respectively.
L000143	\label{fig:Int}
L000144	}
L000145	
L000146	\end{figure}
L000147	A prototypical discrete evolution equation is
L000148	\begin{equation}\label{eq:adiabatic_dynamics}
L000149	    X_{n+1} = \sV X_{n},
L000150	\end{equation}
L000151	where the vector $X_n\in\mathbb{C}^N$ characterizes the state of the system after $n$ time steps, for example, in terms of a probability distribution, and $\sV\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times N}$ is the one-step propagator or generator.
L000152	Such first-order recurrence relations provide suitable models for systems whose future is, at any point in time, fully determined by their present state.
L000153	This condition is satisfied if all relevant degrees of freedom are accessible to the observer.
L000154	In particular in mesoscopic systems, however, it is typically only possible to resolve coarse grained  variables, while details of internal and environmental structures remain inaccessible~\cite{pigolottiCoarseGrainingMaster2008,
L000155	espositoStochasticThermodynamicsCoarse2012,
L000156	yvinecAdiabaticReductionModel2014,
L000157	hummerOptimalDimensionalityReduction2015,
L000158	wachtelThermodynamicallyConsistentCoarse2018,
L000159	lapollaManifestationsProjectionInducedMemory2019,
L000160	strasbergNonMarkovianityNegativeEntropy2019,
L000161	busielloCoarsegrainedEntropyProduction2020,
L000162	seiferthCoarseGrainingBiochemical2020,
L000163	loosIrreversibilityHeatAndInformation2020,
L000164	straubeRapidOnset2020,
L000165	ayazNonMarkovianModelingProtein2021,
L000166	hartichEmergentMemoryKinetic2021,
L000167	lapollaToolboxQuantifyingMemory2021,
L000168	hartichViolationLocalDetailed2023,
L000169	coghiCurrentFluctuations2024,
L000170	blomMilestoningEstimatorsDissipation2024,
L000171	vollmarModelfreeInferenceMemory2024,
L000172	zhaoEmergenceMemoryEquilibrium2024,
L000173	wisniewskiMemoryinducedCurrentReversal2025a}.
L000174	These hidden degrees of freedom can, if they evolve sufficiently slowly, retain information about the observable system over several time steps, while constantly influencing its dynamics.
L000175	Accounting for this effect requires more general evolution equations of the form
L000176	\begin{equation}
L000177	    \label{eq:starting_point}
L000178	    X_{n+1} = \sV X_n + \sum\nolimits_{m=1}^{n} \sK_m X_{n-m},
L000179	\end{equation}
L000180	where the memory kernel $\sK_n$ can either be extracted from experimental or simulation data, or derived by projecting the state space of a microscopic model onto a lower-dimensional space of mesostates~\cite{lesnickiMolecularHydrodynamics2016, jungIterativeReconstruction2017, meyerNumericalProcedure2020, vrugtProjectionOperatorsStatistical2020,
L000181	klippensteinIntroducingMemoryCoarseGrained2021,
L000182	schillingCoarsegrainedModellingOut2022,
L000183	hilderQuantitativeCoarseGrainingMarkov2024}.
L000184	We assume throughout that neither the one-step propagator nor the memory the kernel depend explicitly on time.
L000185	This condition is satisfied whenever the underlying micro-dynamics are stroboscopically homogeneous, as is the case for stationary and periodically driven processes.
L000186	
L000187	
L000188	Higher-order recurrence relations of the type of Eq.~\eqref{eq:starting_point} can provide significantly more accurate models for systems with hidden degrees of freedom than their first-order counterparts, especially when high temporal resolution is required and memory effects cannot be suppressed by controlling the time step.
L000189	However, they are also less physically transparent and more difficult to solve both analytically and numerically, especially when the accessible state space remains large.
L000190	Here, we show how this trade-off can be systematically overcome in settings where memory effects play a subdominant role.
L000191	To this end, we reconstruct a recently developed theory of continuous weak-memory in discrete time, thereby substantially broadening the scope of this framework~\cite{
L000192	brandnerDynamicsMicroscaleNanoscale2025a,
L000193	brandnerDynamicsMicroscaleNanoscale2025}.
L000194	
L000195	The key idea of our approach is to construct an effective generator $\sG\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times N}$ such that the solution $Y_n\in\mathbb{C}^N$ of the first-order recurrence relation
L000196	\begin{equation}\label{eq:LTApprox}
L000197		Y_{n+1} = \sG Y_n
L000198	\end{equation}
L000199	converges to the solution of Eq.~\eqref{eq:starting_point} at sufficiently long times.
L000200	The short-time dynamics of the system are described as an effectively instantaneous change of its initial state, $Y_0 = \sD X_0$, induced by the slippage matrix $\sD \in \mathbb{C}^{N \times N}$, which, for any $X_0$, has to satisfy
L000201	\begin{equation}\label{eq:SMAsymp}
L000202		\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty} \sG^{-n} X_n = \sD X_0.
L000203	\end{equation}
L000204	Here, $\sG$ and $\sD$ must be non-singular to ensure that the long-time approximation $Y_n$ is non-trivial for any $X_0\neq 0$.
L000205	The physical intuition behind this ansatz is that, after a brief transient period, all hidden degrees of freedom reach a near-stationary state, which then evolves almost adiabatically under the influence of the observable ones.
L000206	As a result, the backflow of information from inaccessible to accessible components of the system gradually diminishes and the impact of memory effects becomes insignificant.
L000207	
L000208	The validity of this picture depends on three characteristic time scales, which we define by imposing the bounds
L000209	\begin{equation}\label{eq:WMC1}
L000210		 \nrm{\sV^{-1}}\leq 1/v, \qquad \nrm{\sK_n}\leq Mk^{n-1}
L000211	\end{equation}
L000212	on the free generator and the memory kernel, where $\nrm{\cdot}$ denotes the spectral norm.
L000213	Here, $v$ corresponds to the slowest mode of the adiabatic dynamics, which would be observed if the evolution of all hidden degrees of freedom was frozen.
L000214	The parameters $M$ and $k < 1$ quantify, respectively, the coupling strength between accessible and inaccessible parts of the system and the relaxation rate of the latter.
L000215	Thus, when $M$ and $k$ are small compared to $v$, the hidden degrees of freedom evolve nearly autonomously, with the observable ones acting only as a weak and slow perturbation.
L000216	Under these conditions, memory effects are expected to be subdominant and an effective generator satisfying Eq.~\eqref{eq:SMAsymp} may exist.
L000217	
L000218	Our main achievement is to translate the qualitative analysis above into a mathematical theorem, which can be formulated as follows.
L000219	If the parameters $v, M$ and $k$ entering the bounds \eqref{eq:WMC1} satisfy
L000220	\begin{equation}\label{eq:WMC2}
L000221		k<v, \quad
L000222		M < M^\ast =
L000223			\begin{cases}
L000224				(v-k)^2/4, & v+k <2\\
L000225				(1-k)(v-1),& v+k \geq 2
L000226			\end{cases},
L000227	\end{equation}
L000228	there exists a unique effective generator $\sG$ such that, for any initial state $X_0$, the long-time approximation $Y_n$ of the solution $X_n$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:starting_point} satisfies
L000229	\begin{equation}\label{eq:BndErrLTA}
L000230		\abs{X_n - Y_n}\leq \frac{\zeta-k}{\eta-\zeta}\abs{X_0}\zeta^n
L000231	\end{equation}
L000232	with
L000233	\begin{align}
L000234		\zeta & = (v+k)/2 - \sqrt{(v-k)^2/4 -M} < 1,\\
L000235		\eta & = (v+k)/2 + \sqrt{(v-k)^2/4- M}
L000236	\end{align}
L000237	and $\abs{\cdot}$ indicating the Euclidean norm.
L000238	The corresponding slippage matrix is given by the formula
L000239	\begin{equation}\label{eq:SMDef}
L000240		\sD^{-1} = 1 + \sum\nolimits_{m=1}^\infty\sum\nolimits_{n=1}^\infty \sH^{-m}\sK_{m+n-1}\sG^{-n},
L000241	\end{equation}
L000242	where $\sG$ and $\sH$ are the unique solutions of the fixed-point equations
L000243	\begin{align}
L000244		\label{eq:GFPE}
L000245		\sG & = \sV + \sum\nolimits_{n=1}^\infty \sK_n \sG^{-n} = T(\sG),\\
L000246		\label{eq:HFPE}
L000247		\sH & = \sV + \sum\nolimits_{n=1}^\infty \sH^{-n}\sK_n  = U(\sH)
L000248	\end{align}
L000249	in the set $	B=\bigl\{\sX\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times N} \; : \; \nrm{\sX^{-1}}\leq 1/\eta\bigr\}$.
L000250	
L000251	
L000252	The proof of this theorem and the required mathematical methods are discussed in the Supplemental Material \cite{meyerWeakMemoryDynamicsDiscrete2025}\nocite{agarwalFixedPointTheory2018,hornMatrixAnalysis2013,rivasOpenQuantumSystems2012,
L000253	burtonVolterraIntegralDifferential2005}. Here, we only add three remarks. First, Eqs.~\eqref{eq:WMC1} and \eqref{eq:WMC2} define the weak-memory regime for discrete dynamics, where our theory is applicable without further restrictions.
L000254	These conditions do not require a strong separation of time scales, but can be met even when $M, k$ and $v$ are of the same order of magnitude.
L000255	In such situations, phenomenological methods such as the standard Born-Markov approximation are typically not reliable \cite{strunzConvolutionlessNonMarkovian2004, flemingAccuracyPerturbativeMaster2011, hartmannAccuracyAssessmentPerturbative2020, tellobreuerBenchmarkingQuantumMaster2024, sartipiCanonicallyConsistentQuantumMaster2026}.
L000256	
L000257	Second, Eq.~\eqref{eq:BndErrLTA} provides a strong bound on the error of the long-time approximation, thereby enforcing the uniqueness of the effective generator.
L000258	That is, while there may exist a whole family of generators that define a non-singular slippage matrix, there is only one such pair whose corresponding long-time approximation satisfies
L000259	\begin{equation}
L000260		\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty} \abs{X_n - Y_n}\zeta^{-n} < \infty
L000261	\end{equation}
L000262	for any $X_0$.
L000263	Finally, Eqs.~\eqref{eq:SMDef}, \eqref{eq:GFPE} and \eqref{eq:HFPE} provide an explicit scheme to construct this optimal approximation to arbitrary accuracy, which would not be possible with standard weak-coupling expansions whose convergence properties are not generally understood.
L000264	To this end, the fixed-point equations \eqref{eq:GFPE} and \eqref{eq:HFPE} may be solved with a suitable ansatz or by iteration, where the latter approach exploits the fact that $T$ and $U$ are contractions on $B$ under the metric $d(\sX,\sY)=\nrm{\sX-\sY}$.
L000265	
L000266	As a first application, we show how theory can be employed in the coarse graining of Markov processes.
L000267	We focus on systems with a finite space of microstates $\mathbb{M}=\{1,\dots,M\}$ and denote by $P_n=[P_n^1,\dots,P_n^M]$ the time dependent vector that assigns an occupation probability to every microstate.
L000268	Dynamics are governed by the discrete-time master equation
L000269	$P_{n+1} = \sL P_n$,
L000270	where $(\sL)_{ij}\geq 0$ is the probability to transition from the microstates $i$ to the microstate $j$ in one step.
L000271	A coarse graining is induced by a partitioning of $\mathbb{M}$ into $N$ disjoint subsets $\mathbb{M}_\alpha$, which correspond to the operationally distinguishable configurations of the system.
L000272	The occupation probabilities $X_n^\alpha = \sum_{i\in\mathbb{M}_\alpha}P^i_n$ of these mesostates are collected in the lumped vector $X_n=[X_n^1,\dots,X_n^N]$.
L000273	Under minor technical conditions, this vector follows an evolution equation of the form \eqref{eq:starting_point}, where $\sV$ and $\sK_n$ can be expressed in terms of the microscopic transition matrix $\sL$ using projection operator techniques~\cite{vrugtProjectionOperatorsStatistical2020,
L000274	klippensteinIntroducingMemoryCoarseGrained2021,
L000275	schillingCoarsegrainedModellingOut2022,
L000276	hilderQuantitativeCoarseGrainingMarkov2024}.
L000277	
L000278	Our master theorem extends this framework by establishing rigorous conditions for the existence of, and systematic procedures to construct, an effective generator of the lumped dynamics.
L000279	These results apply to broad classes of physical systems, including in particular ones with continuous underlying dynamics.
L000280	Systems such as electronic nano-devices, for example, are typically  described by a continuous-time master equation~\cite{seifertStochasticThermodynamicsFluctuation2012,
L000281	benentiFundamentalAspectsSteadystate2017},
L000282	$\dot{p}_t = \sW_t p_t.$
L000283	Here, $p_t = [p^1_t,\dots, p^M_t]^\trans$ is a vector of occupation probabilities, $(\sW_t)_{ij} \geq 0$ is the transition rate from the micro\-state $j$ to the micro\-state $i$, which becomes time dependent under external driving, and $(\sW_t)_{ii} = -\sum_{j\neq i} (\sW_t)_{ji}$ to ensure probability conservation.
L000284	If the driving is periodic, $\sW_{t+\tau} = \sW_t$, Floquet's theorem implies that the stroboscopic vector $P_n = p_{n\tau}$ follows a discrete-time master equation with the transition matrix $\sL = \exp_{\mathrm{O}}[\int_0^\tau dt\;  \sW_t]$.
L000285	This observation shows that our theory opens a path to systematically derive effective generators for coarse-grained, periodically modulated dynamics, a task that could not be addressed with the existing weak-memory theory for continuous dynamics, and, more generally, poses a significant challenge without additional assumptions on the driving time scale or the use of uncontrolled approximations \cite{mozgunovCompletelyPositiveMaster2020,
L000286	nathanUniversalLindbladEquation2020,
L000287	schnellThereFloquetLindbladian2020,
L000288	hotzCoarsegrainingMasterEquation2021,
L000289	moriStrongMarkovDissipation2025}.
L000290	
L000291	Two remarks are in order before we move on to a simple example.
L000292	First, if $\sL$ is a stochastic matrix, as assumed above, then so is the free generator $\sV$ of the lumped dynamics.
L000293	Consequently, the Perron-Frobenius theorem implies $v\leq 1$ for this entire class of models.
L000294	Second, while we have so far focused on classical Markov jump processes, our conclusions extend directly to open quantum systems with a finite-dimensional Hilbert space, upon replacing probability vectors, transition matrices, and rate matrices by density matrices, quantum channels, and Lindblad generators, respectively.
L000295	
L000296	\begin{figure}
L000297	    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{F2.pdf}
L000298	    \caption{Mesoscopic charge pump.
L000299	    \textbf{Left:}
L000300	    For $L_+ = L_- = L$, the shaded area indicates the region in the parameter space of the model,
L000301	    where the weak-memory conditions \eqref{eq:WMC1} and \eqref{eq:WMC2} are satisfied with
L000302	    $v=1/\nrm{\sV^{-1}}$ and $M=\nrm{\sK_1}$.
L000303	    \textbf{Right:}
L000304	    Error of the long-time approximation $Y_n=\sG^n\sD X_0$ with respect to the exact solution
L000305	    $X_n$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:starting_point} (solid), for $X_0 = [1,0]^\trans$ and selected
L000306	    parameter sets, indicated with dots in the left panel.
L000307	    For comparison, we also show the error bound \eqref{eq:BndErrLTA} (dashed).
L000308	    }
L000309	    \label{fig:Cpm}
L000310	\end{figure}
L000311	
L000312	We now focus on the charge-pump model of Fig.~\ref{fig:Int}.
L000313	A complete operation cycle consists of the three strokes shown in panels (a), (b), (c), which are described by the stochastic matrices $\sL_+$, $\sL_0$, $\sL_-$, respectively.
L000314	The microscopic transition matrix is given by $\sL=\sL_-\sL_0\sL_+$.
L000315	For an observer monitoring only the total charge of the two quantum dots, the system is described by the lumped probability vector $X_n = [X^\circ_n, X^\bullet_n]^\trans$, where $X^\circ_n = P^{\circ\circ}_n$ and $X^\bullet_n = P^{\circ\bullet}_n + P^{\bullet\circ}_n$.
L000316	If the initial charge distribution is symmetric, as we assume here only for the sake of simplicity, $P^{\circ\bullet}_0 = P^{\bullet\circ}_0$, this vector follows Eq.~\eqref{eq:starting_point} with
L000317	\begin{align}
L000318		\sV & = 1 + L_+(1-L_0L_-)\sJ_1 + \frac{L_-}{2}\sJ_2,\\
L000319		\sK_n & = \frac{L_+L_-(1-2L_0+2L_-)k^n}{2-L_-}\sJ_1
L000320			- \frac{L_-^ 2k^n}{2(2-L_-)}\sJ_2,
L000321	\end{align}
L000322	where $k= (1-2L_0)(2-L_-)/2$,
L000323	$\sJ_1 = \big[\begin{smallmatrix} -1 & 0\\ \hphantom{-}1 & 0 \end{smallmatrix}\big]$,
L000324	$\sJ_2 = \big[\begin{smallmatrix} 0 & \hphantom{-}1\\ 0 & -1 \end{smallmatrix}\big]$,
L000325	and $L_+$, $L_0$ and $L_-$ are the absorption, tunneling and ejection probabilities as defined in Fig.~\ref{fig:Int}.
L000326	For $L_0 = 1/2$, the charge distribution fully equilibrates in one cycle and the memory kernel vanishes.
L000327	Away from this limit, the parameters $L_\pm$, which are determined by the coupling strengths between quantum dots and reservoirs, must remain sufficiently small for the weak-memory conditions \eqref{eq:WMC1} and \eqref{eq:WMC2} to hold, see Fig.~\ref{fig:Cpm}.
L000328	In this regime, the fixed-point equation \eqref{eq:GFPE} for the effective generator can be solved using the ansatz $\sG = 1 + g_1\sJ_1 + g_2\sJ_2$, and the adjoint generator $\sH$ follows analogously.
L000329	The long-time approximation $Y_n = \sG^n\sD X_0$ is then obtained using the formula \eqref{eq:SMDef} for the slippage matrix $\sD$~\cite{meyerWeakMemoryDynamicsDiscrete2025}.
L000330	As Fig.~\ref{fig:Cpm} shows, the error of this approximation decays exponentially with $n$, closely following the upper bound \eqref{eq:BndErrLTA}.
L000331	
L000332	\begin{figure}
L000333	    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{F3.pdf}
L000334	    \caption{Collisional model.
L000335	    \textbf{Left:}
L000336	    Weak-memory regime in the parameter space defined by the swap probabilities $u$ and $k$,
L000337	    with $v = (1-u)(1-k)$ and $M=k(1-k)$.
L000338	    The ancillas are initially in fully mixed states.
L000339	    \textbf{Right:}
L000340	    Error of the long-time approximation \eqref{eq:LTACM} for different approximate generators
L000341	    $\mG_\ell = T(\mG_{\ell-1})$ (solid), together with the bound
L000342	    \eqref{eq:BndLTACM} (dashed).
L000343	    For all plots, we used the model parameters indicated by the dot in the left panel, and
L000344	    set $\rho_0 = \ket{0_S}\bra{0_S}$.
L000345	    \label{fig:Clm}
L000346	    }
L000347	\end{figure}
L000348	
L000349	As our second main application, we consider collisional models, which offer a versatile platform to describe open or monitored quantum dynamics in discrete time~\cite{ciccarelloQuantumCollisionModels2022}.
L000350	As illustrated in Fig.~1, such models consist of a system of interest that couples sequentially to a collection of identical environmental units, or ancillas.
L000351	Memory effects can be added to this framework, for instance, through interactions between successive ancillas.
L000352	The state of the system after $n$ collisions is then given by
L000353	\begin{equation}
L000354		\rho_n = \tr_A[\mU_n \;\cdots\; \mU_2\mQ_{12}\mU_1(\rho_0 \otimes\xi^n)],
L000355	\end{equation}
L000356	where $\xi^n = \bigotimes_{i=1}^n\xi_i$ is the initial state of the ancillas, and $\tr_A[\circ]$ indicates the partial trace over their joint Hilbert space.
L000357	The maps $\mU_i$ and $\mQ_{ij}$ describe bipartite interactions between the $i^\mathrm{th}$ ancilla and the system, and between the $i^\mathrm{th}$ and the $j^\mathrm{th}$ ancillas, respectively.
L000358	
L000359	For concreteness, we now assume that the system and the ancillas are qubits with ground and excited states $\ket{0_i}$ and $\ket{1_i}$, where $i=S,1,\dots,n$.
L000360	System-ancilla and ancilla-ancilla interactions are described as unitary and incoherent partial swaps,
L000361	\begin{equation}
L000362		\mU_i^{\vphantom{\dagger}}\circ = \sU_i^{\vphantom{\dagger}}\circ\sU_i^\dagger, \quad
L000363		\mQ_{ij}^{\vphantom{\dagger}}\circ= (1-k)\circ
L000364			+ k\sS_{ij}^{\vphantom{\dagger}}\circ\sS_{ij}^{\vphantom{\dagger}},
L000365	\end{equation}
L000366	where $\sU_i = \sqrt{1-u} - \mathrm{i} \sqrt{u}\sS_{Si}$ and $0<u,k<1$.
L000367	The swap matrices are defined such that $\sS_{ij}\ket{\alpha_i,\beta_j} = \ket{\beta_i,\alpha_j}$ for $\alpha,\beta\in\{0,1\}$.
L000368	If all ancillas are initially prepared in the same state, the dynamics of this model are governed by the recursion relation
L000369	\begin{equation}\label{eq:EOMCM}
L000370		\rho_{n+1} = \mK_0 \rho_n + \sum\nolimits_{m=1}^n \mK_{m}\rho_{n-m} + \mF_{n+1}\rho_0,
L000371	\end{equation}
L000372	with $\mK_n = (1/k-1)\mF_{n+1}$ and $\mF_n\circ = k^n\tr_1^{\vphantom{n}}[\mU^n_1(\circ\otimes\xi_1^{\vphantom{n}})]$, see Refs.~\cite{meyerWeakMemoryDynamicsDiscrete2025,ciccarelloQuantumCollisionModels2022}.
L000373	Since $\mF_n$ is a linear map on the operator space of the system, it can be represented as a four-dimensional matrix \footnote{In our discussion of collisional models, we formally treat density matrices as vectors and quantum maps as matrices.
L000374	For this approach to be consistent, $\abs{\cdot}$ must, when applied to a density matrix, be evaluated as the Hilbert-Schmidt norm; $\nrm{\cdot}$ denotes the operator norm induced by $\abs{\cdot}$ throughout.}.
L000375	Thus, Eq.~\eqref{eq:EOMCM} has the structure of Eq.~\eqref{eq:starting_point}, up to an inhomogeneous term, which can, however, be accounted for.
L000376	If $\mG$ and $\mD$ are the effective generator and slippage map of the homogeneous dynamics, then the long-time approximation of $\rho_n$ reads~\cite{meyerWeakMemoryDynamicsDiscrete2025}
L000377	\begin{equation}\label{eq:LTACM}
L000378		\chi_n = \mG^n\mD\rho_0 + \sum\nolimits_{m=1}^n\mG^{n-m}\mD\mF_m\rho_0.
L000379	\end{equation}
L000380	To evaluate this expression, we solve the fixed-point equations \eqref{eq:GFPE} and \eqref{eq:HFPE} numerically by iteration using the initial guesses $\mG_0 = \mH_0 = \mK_0$; the corresponding slippage map then follows from Eq.~\eqref{eq:SMDef}.
L000381	As we show in Fig.~\ref{fig:Clm}, the error of the resulting approximations first decays exponentially with time, obeying the bound
L000382	\begin{equation}\label{eq:BndLTACM}
L000383		\abs{\rho_n-\chi_n}\leq \frac{\zeta-k}{\eta-\zeta}\abs{\rho_0}
L000384			\zeta^n\Bigl(1 + \sum\nolimits_{m=1}^n \nrm{\mF_m}\zeta^{-m}\Bigr),
L000385	\end{equation}
L000386	which follows from Eq.~\eqref{eq:BndErrLTA} and holds for the exact effective generator and slippage map~\cite{meyerWeakMemoryDynamicsDiscrete2025}, before approaching a plateau whose value decreases exponentially with the number of iterations used to determine the generator.
L000387	
L000388	This observation shows that iterating the fixed-point equations \eqref{eq:GFPE} and \eqref{eq:HFPE} provides an efficient numerical method for determining the effective generator and the associated slippage matrix.
L000389	However, due to their nonlinear structure, these equations generally do not provide a convenient starting point for analytical perturbation schemes.
L000390	For this purpose, it is more instructive to focus instead on the $\sE_n\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times N}$, which, for any given $\sG$, is uniquely defined by requiring that the inhomogeneous recurrence relation
L000391	\begin{equation}
L000392		X_{n+1} = \sG X_n + \sE_n X_0
L000393	\end{equation}
L000394	is equivalent to Eq.~\eqref{eq:starting_point}.
L000395	If the weak-memory conditions \eqref{eq:WMC1} and \eqref{eq:WMC2} are fulfilled, and $\sG$ is the optimal effective generator of our master theorem, this function satisfies the linear summation equation
L000396	\begin{equation}
L000397		\sE_n = -\sum\nolimits_{m=n+1}^\infty\Bigl(
L000398			\sK_{m} + \sum\nolimits_{l=1}^{m-1}\sE_{l-1}\sK_{m-l}\Bigr)\sV^{n-m},
L000399	\end{equation}
L000400	which can be solved by iteration.
L000401	In this way, the optimal memory function $\sE_n$ of a given model can be systematically constructed from an initial guess $\sE^0_n$.
L000402	This initial guess may either be set to zero, or derived from an exact solution of Eq.~\eqref{eq:starting_point} for a suitable limiting case of the model.
L000403	If the free solution satisfies the bound $\nrm{\sE^0_n}\leq S\sigma^n$ with $\zeta\leq\sigma<\min\{\eta,1\}$ and $S\geq \sigma -k$, the iteration converges.
L000404	Once the optimal memory function has been determined, the corresponding effective generator and slippage matrix follow from the relations
L000405	\begin{equation}
L000406	\sG = \sV - \sE_0, \qquad
L000407	\sD = 1 + \sum\nolimits_{n=1}^\infty \sG^{-n}\sE_{n-1}.
L000408	\end{equation}
L000409	For the trivial initial guess $\sE^0_n = 0$, for instance, we obtain an expansion of $\sG$ in powers of the coupling strength $M$.
L000410	
L000411	These results, whose derivation we provide in Ref.~\cite{meyerWeakMemoryDynamicsDiscrete2025}, complete our theory of weak memory effects in discrete time.
L000412	Before closing, we stress that, while the above framework achieves a high degree of structural uniformity with the one recently developed for continuous dynamics, our results could not be derived from that theory, since the exact discretization of evolution equations with memory is a non-trivial procedure. In particular, there is no simple correspondence between the discrete one-step propagator and memory kernel and their continuous counterparts.
L000413	More generally, our work provides a universal basis to explore the potentially rich physics of dynamical memory in settings with inherently discrete dynamics, such as many-body circuit and collisional models.
L000414	On the applied side, it offers new tools for the extrapolation of short-time data obtained from experiments or molecular dynamics simulations \cite{jungIterativeReconstructionMemory2017,
L000415	klippensteinIntroducingMemoryCoarseGrained2021a,
L000416	vroylandtLikelihoodbasedNonMarkovianModels2022,
L000417	tepperAccurateMemoryKernel2024,
L000418	daltonMemoryFrictionNanoscale2025,
L000419	satoreMarkovTypeStateModels2025}.
L000420	The applicability of our framework is determined solely by the weak-memory conditions \eqref{eq:WMC1} and \eqref{eq:WMC2}.
L000421	It thus extends beyond the standard weak-coupling and quasi-Markovian limits, which impose stronger and less rigorous constraints on system parameters.
L000422	At the same time, our results provide a well-defined starting point for future research seeking to systematically characterize strong-memory dynamics.
