L000890	
L000891	\subsection{Minimal Model}
L000892	
L000893	We now turn to the mesoscopic charge pump discussed in the main text.
L000894	In continuous time, the device is described by the master equation
L000895	\begin{equation}
L000896	\dot{p}_t = \sW_t p_t,
L000897	\end{equation}
L000898	where $p_t = [p^{\circ\circ}_t, p^{\bullet\circ}_t, p^{\circ\bullet}_t]^\sT$ denotes the microscopic probability vector and the time-dependent rate matrix is defined as
L000899	\begin{equation}
L000900	\sW_t = \left\{
L000901	\begin{array}{llll}
L000902	\sW_+, & 0      \!\! & \leq \;\; t\!\! \mod\tau \;\; < &\tau_+\\
L000903	\sW_0, & \tau_+ \!\! & \leq \;\; t\!\! \mod\tau \;\; < &\tau_+ + \tau_0\\
L000904	\sW_-, & \tau_+ +\tau_0 \!\! & \leq \;\; t\!\! \mod\tau \;\;< & \tau_++\tau_0+\tau_- = \tau
L000905	\end{array}
L000906	\right.
L000907	\end{equation}
L000908	with the rate matrices
L000909	\begin{equation}
L000910	\sW_+= \left[
L000911	\begin{array}{rrr}
L000912	- w_+ & 0 & 0\\
L000913	w_+ & 0 & 0\\
L000914	0   & 0 & 0
L000915	\end{array}
L000916	\right], \qquad
L000917	\sW_0= \left[
L000918	\begin{array}{rrr}
L000919	0 & 0 & 0\\
L000920	0 & -w_0 &  w_0\\
L000921	0 &  w_0 & -w_0
L000922	\end{array}
L000923	\right], \qquad
L000924	\sW_-= \left[
L000925	\begin{array}{rrr}
L000926	0 & 0 & w_-\\
L000927	0 & 0 & 0\\
L000928	0 & 0 & -w_-
L000929	\end{array}
L000930	\right]
L000931	\end{equation}
L000932	representing the three strokes of the operation cycle.
L000933	Thus, the transition matrix that describes the evolution of the stroboscopic probability vector $P_n = p_{n\tau} = [P^{\circ\circ}_n, P^{\bullet\circ}_n, P^{\circ\bullet}_n]^\trans$ takes the form $\sL = e^{\sW_-\tau_-}e^{\sW_0\tau_0}e^{\sW_+\tau_+} = \sL_-\sL_0\sL_+$, and the parameters introduced in the main text are given by $L_\pm= 1 -e^{-w_\pm\tau_\pm}$ and $L_0 = (1-e^{-2w_0\tau_0})/2$.
L000934	We now fix the reference probability vectors $R_\circ = [1]$ and $R_\bullet = [1/2,1/2]^\trans$ for the two mesostates $\circ$ and $\bullet$, which correspond to the conductor being neutral and occupied by a single charge, respectively.
L000935	Using the fomalism outlined in the previous section, it is then straightforward to derive the expressions
L000936	\begin{equation}
L000937	\sV = 1+ L_+(1-L_0L_-)\sJ_1 + \frac{L_-}{2}\sJ_2, \qquad
L000938	\sK_n = \frac{L_+L_-(1-2L_0+2L_-)k^n}{2-L_-}\sJ_1
L000939	- \frac{L_-^2k^n}{2(2-L_-)}\sJ_2
L000940	\end{equation}
L000941	for the free generator and the memory kernel, where $k=(1-2L_0)(2-L_-)/2$ and we have introduced the matrices
L000942	\begin{equation}
L000943	\sJ_1 = \left[\begin{array}{rr}
L000944	-1 & 0 \\ 1 & 0
L000945	\end{array}
L000946	\right], \qquad
L000947	\sJ_2 = \left[\begin{array}{rr}
L000948	0 & 1 \\ 0 & -1
L000949	\end{array}
L000950	\right],
L000951	\end{equation}
L000952	which satisfy the multiplication rules $\sJ_1\sJ_2= -\sJ_2$ and $\sJ_2\sJ_1= -\sJ_1$.
L000953	For simplicity, we assume that $P^{\circ\bullet}_0 = P^{\bullet\circ}_0$ such that the inhomogeneous term in the effective evolution equation \eqref{eq:sp:NLEEQ} vanishes.
L000954	Since the memory kernel has the simple structure $\sK_n = k^{n-1}\sK_1$, the non-linear fixed-point equations \eqref{eq:GFPE} and \eqref{eq:HFPE} of the main text reduce to the quadratic matrix equations
L000955	\begin{equation}
L000956	\sG^2 -(k+\sV)\sG + k\sV - \sK_1 = 0, \qquad
L000957	\sH^2 -\sH(k+\sV) + k\sV - \sK_1 = 0.
L000958	\end{equation}
L000959	The effective generator $\sG$ and its adjoint counterpart $\sH$ can therefore be obtained by setting $\sG = 1 + g_1 \sJ_1 + g_2 \sJ_2$ and $\sH = 1 + h_1 \sJ_1 + h_2 \sJ_2$ and solving the resulting quadratic equations for the parameters $g_1, g_2$ and $h_1, h_2$.
L000960	The slippage matrix $\sD$ then follows from Eq.~\eqref{eq:SMDef} of the main text.
L000961	
L000962	
L000963	\section{Application 2: Collisional Model}
L000964	
L000965	This section provides further details on the quantum collisional model discussed in the main text, following Ref.~[78] of the main text.
L000966	A general collisional model consists of a system of interest and $N$ ancillas.
L000967	The initial density matrix of this setup is given by
L000968	\begin{equation}
L000969	\sigma_0 = \rho_0 \otimes \xi^1 \otimes \cdots \otimes \xi^N,
L000970	\end{equation}
L000971	where $\rho_0$ and $\xi^{1},\dots,\xi^N$ are the initial states of the system and the ancillas, respectively.
L000972	Dynamics are generated by coupling the ancillas one by one to the system.
L000973	To introduce memory effects, we further include interactions between consecutive ancillas.
L000974	The state $\sigma_n$ of the full system after $n\leq N$ collisions then satisfies the recursion relation
L000975	\begin{equation}
L000976	\sigma_{n+1}   = \mU_{n+1} \mQ_{n,n+1}\sigma_n,
L000977	\end{equation}
L000978	where the bipartite maps $\mU_i$ and $\mQ_{ij}$ describe interactions between the system proper and the $i^\mathrm{th}$ ancilla, and between the $i^\mathrm{th}$ and the $j^\mathrm{th}$ ancilla, respectively.
L000979	Here, we use the convention $\mQ_{10}=\mathcal{I}$.
L000980	
L000981	Deriving a closed equation of motion for the state $\rho_n = \tr_A[\sigma_n]$ of the system, where $\tr_A[\circ]$ indicates the partial trace over the joint Hilbert space of the ancillas, is in general not straightforward.
L000982	For simplicity, we therefore focus on a specific model, where both the system and the ancillas are qubits.
L000983	System-ancilla and ancilla-ancilla interactions are described by the unitary and incoherent partial swap maps
L000984	\begin{equation}
L000985	\mU_i\circ = \sU_i^{\vphantom{\dagger}}\circ\sU_i^\dagger, \qquad
L000986	\mQ_{ij}\circ = (1-k)\circ+k\sS_{ij}\circ\sS_{ij}
L000987	\end{equation}
L000988	with $\sU_i=\exp[-\mathrm{i}\theta\sS_{Si}]=\sqrt{1-u}-\mathrm{i}\sqrt{u}\sS_{Si}$, where $u=\sin^ 2[\theta]$ and $0\leq \theta\leq\pi/2$.
L000989	Here, $0<u,k<1$ are the swap probabilities and the Hermitian swap matrices are defined such that $\sS_{ij}\ket{\alpha_i,\beta_j}=\ket{\beta_i,\alpha_j}$, where $i,j=S,1,\dots,N$ and $\alpha,\beta =0,1$; the states $\ket{0_S}, \ket{1_S}$ and $\ket{0_i}, \ket{1_i}$ form the canonical bases of the system and the $i^\mathrm{th}$ ancilla, respectively.
L000990	Provided that all ancillas are initially prepared in the same state, $\xi^i = \xi^1$, one can now show that the state of the system follows the evolution equation
L000991	\begin{equation}\label{eq:cm:NLEEQ}
L000992	\rho_{n+1} = \mV\rho_n + \sum_{m=1}^n\mK_m\rho_{n-m}+\mF_{n+1}\rho_0,
L000993	\end{equation}
L000994	where $\mV=\mK_0$, $\mK_n= (1/k -1)\mF_{n+1}$, and $\mF_n\circ=k^n\tr_1^{\vphantom{n}}[\mU_1^n(\circ\otimes\xi^1)]$, for details see Ref.~[78] of the main text.
L000995	
L000996	This recurrence relation formally has the same structure as Eq.~\eqref{eq:mt:NLEEq}, for which our general theory was developed, up to an inhomogeneous term, which can be accommodated as follows.
L000997	We first note that the solution of Eq.~\eqref{eq:cm:NLEEQ} is given by the variation of parameters formula (see Ref. [65] of the main text)
L000998	\begin{equation}
L000999	\rho_n = \mZ_n \rho_0 + \sum_{m=1}^n\mZ_{n-m}\mF_m\rho_0,
L001000	\end{equation}
L001001	where $\mZ_n$ denotes the free propagator, which satisfies
L001002	\begin{equation}
L001003	\mZ_{n+1} = \mV\mZ_n + \sum_{m=1}^n\mK_m\mZ_{n-m}, \qquad \mZ_0=\mathcal{I}.
L001004	\end{equation}
L001005	Thus, upon inserting the long-time approximation $\mY_n = \mG^n \mD$ of the propagator, we obtain the expression
L001006	\begin{equation}
L001007	\chi_n = \mG^n\mD\rho_0 + \sum_{m=1}^n\mG^{n-m}\mD\mF_m\rho_0
L001008	\end{equation}
L001009	for the long-time approximation $\chi_n$ of $\rho_n$, where $\mG$ is the effective generator and $\mD$ the slippage map.
L001010	The error of this approximation is subject to the bound
L001011	\begin{align}
L001012	\abs{\rho_n-\chi_n} & \leq \abs{\rho_n - \mG^n\mD\rho_0}
L001013	+ \sum_{m=1}^n\abs{\mZ_{n-m}\mF_m\rho_0-\mG^{m-n}\mD\mF_m\rho_0}\\
L001014	& \leq \frac{\zeta-k}{\eta-\zeta}\abs{\rho_0}\zeta^n + \frac{\zeta-k}{\eta-\zeta}\abs{\rho_0}\zeta^n\sum_{m=1}^n\nrm{\mF_m}\zeta^{-m}.\nonumber
L001015	\end{align}
L001016	Here, we have used that, according to our master theorem, $\nrm{\mZ_n-\mG^n\mD}\leq (\zeta-k)\zeta^n/(\eta-\zeta)$ with $\zeta$ and $\eta$ being defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:mt:ZetEta}, and the primary parameters $v,k,M$ must be determined such that the bounds $\nrm{\mV^{-1}}\leq 1/v$ and $\nrm{\mK_n}\leq M k^{n-1}$ are satisfied.
L001017	
L001018	Throughout this section, we implicitly treat density matrices as vectors and quantum maps as matrices.
L001019	These pictures are essentially equivalent for quantum systems with a finite-dimensional Hilbert space, whose density matrices can be represented as finite linear combinations of orthonormal basis vectors in the corresponding operator space.
L001020	For qubits, the canonical operator basis is given by $\bigl\{\mathsf{1}/\sqrt{2},\sigma_x/\sqrt{2},\sigma_y/\sqrt{2},\sigma_z/\sqrt{2}\bigr\}$, where $\sigma_{x,y,z}$ are the usual Pauli matrices.
L001021	In this basis, the map $\mF_n$, which defines both the free generator $\mV$ and the memory kernel $\mK_n$ of the toy model discussed above, takes the matrix form
L001022	\begin{equation}
L001023	\mF_n\equiv \frac{k^n}{2}\left[
L001024	\begin{array}{cccc}
L001025	2         &           0           &           0           & 0\\
L001026	0          & 2\cos^2[n\theta]  & (2\kappa-1)\sin[2n\theta] &   0\\
L001027	0          & (1-2\kappa)\sin[2n\theta] & 2\cos^2[n\theta]  &   0\\
L001028	(2-4\kappa)\sin^ 2[n\theta]    & 0 &       0           &           2\cos^2[n\theta]
L001029	\end{array}
L001030	\right].
L001031	\end{equation}
L001032	Here, we assume that the ancillas are initially in the state $\xi^i = \xi^1= 1/2+(\kappa-1/2)\sigma_z$, where $\kappa=0$ corresponds to the pure ground state and $\kappa=1/2$ to the fully mixed state.
L001033	We stress that, for this approach to be consistent with our general theory, we need to interpret the norm $\abs{\cdot}$, when applied to a density matrix, as the Euclidean norm of the corresponding vector representation in the operator space of the system.
L001034	That is, if mixed quantum states are treated as operators on the Hilbert space of the system, and thus represented as matrices, $\abs{\cdot}$ must be evaluated as the Hilbert-Schmidt norm.
L001035	In either case, $\nrm{\cdot}$ must be interpreted as the operator norm induced by the norm $\abs{\cdot}$.
L001036	The norms of the maps $\mF_n$ and $\mF_n^{-1}$, for example, are given by
L001037	\begin{equation}
L001038	\nrm{\mF_n}= k^n
L001039	\quad\text{and} \quad
L001040	\nrm{\mF_n^{-1}}= \frac{1}{k^n\cos^2[n\theta]}
L001041	\end{equation}
L001042	for $\kappa =1/2$.
L001043	For this specific value of $\kappa$, which is considered in the main text, the parameters $v,M$ become
L001044	\begin{equation}
L001045	v= (1-k)\cos^2[\theta]=(1-k)(1-u)
L001046	\quad\text{and}\quad
L001047	M=k(1-k),
L001048	\end{equation}
L001049	where $u=\sin^2[\theta]$ is the coherent swap probability.
L001050	
L001051	
